<p>Roof damage insurance claims are the backbone of property claims in Dallas. Dallas County has recorded 841 hail reports and 708 thunderstorm-wind reports in the NOAA Storm Events Database, and both perils land on the roof first. A single storm can bruise shingles, lift tabs, bend flashing, and puncture low-slope membranes, and the resulting leaks can spread into ceilings, insulation, and walls.</p>
<p>Because the roof is often the largest single item on a claim, it is also where many disputes start. This page covers the roof types we see around Dallas County, what insurers look for, and how a public adjuster in Dallas can help you document the damage and review the estimate.</p>

<h2>Roof Types Common Around Dallas</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Composition shingles.</strong> The standard roof on brick-veneer homes across Dallas, Garland, Mesquite, Richardson, and Plano. Hail can fracture the mat and knock off granules; wind can crease or tear tabs and break the seal strip.</li>
<li><strong>Older steep-slope roofs.</strong> Tudor and Craftsman houses in the Park Cities and older homes in neighborhoods like Lakewood and Kessler Park may have tile, slate, wood shake, or architectural shingles that are hard to match. Brittle materials can break during repair attempts.</li>
<li><strong>Mid-century ranch roofs.</strong> Lower-pitch roofs in Lake Highlands and Richardson can collect debris and are sensitive to lifted shingles and failed flashing.</li>
<li><strong>Metal roofs.</strong> Standing-seam and exposed-fastener metal panels resist hail punctures, but dents may raise cosmetic damage exclusion questions.</li>
<li><strong>Flat and low-slope commercial roofs.</strong> TPO, modified bitumen, and built-up roofs on offices, apartments, and warehouses. Hail can fracture membranes and crush insulation boards underneath, which may not show from the surface. See <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What Causes Covered Roof Damage</h2>
<p>Covered roof damage usually comes from a sudden event, not gradual deterioration:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hail.</strong> Spring is peak season in Dallas County, with most reports from March through June. Read more on our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Wind.</strong> Straight-line winds and tornadoes lift and remove shingles, peel back membranes, and damage ridge caps. On May 28, 2024, the National Weather Service found winds up to 95 mph in Garland, and NOAA logged a commercial roof torn off in Carrollton (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1186866" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event</a>). See <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Falling trees and limbs.</strong> Large pecans, oaks, and elms are common in older Dallas neighborhoods, and wind or ice can bring them down onto roofs.</li>
<li><strong>Ice and snow load.</strong> Ice storms such as those in February 2023 and January 2025 can add weight and bring down limbs.</li>
</ul>

