<h2>Common Roof Claim Disputes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Age and wear.</strong> The insurer says granule loss, cracking, or curling is from age rather than storm damage.</li>
<li><strong>Repair vs. replacement.</strong> Spot repairs are approved even when shingles are brittle, discontinued, or damaged across many slopes.</li>
<li><strong>Prior storm or late notice.</strong> The carrier points to an earlier event or argues the claim was not reported promptly.</li>
<li><strong>Cosmetic damage.</strong> Metal roof dents are treated as excluded cosmetic damage under an endorsement.</li>
<li><strong>Installation defects.</strong> Leaks are attributed to poor workmanship rather than wind or hail.</li>
</ul>
<p>When the gap is about the cost of repairs rather than coverage, a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement</a> or <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> may be options. If the claim was rejected, see <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Deductibles, Depreciation, and Payment Schedules</h2>
<p>Many Texas policies carry a separate wind/hail deductible, often 1% or 2% of the dwelling limit. Replacement-cost policies typically pay actual cash value first, then release recoverable depreciation after the roof is replaced, often within a time limit stated in the policy. Some policies cover roofs only at actual cash value or under a payment schedule based on roof age. Check your declarations page and endorsements before you sign a roofing contract, so you know what the claim may realistically pay.</p>

<h2>How Dallas Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
<p>Dallas Public Adjuster, a service of Rise Public Adjusting LLC, represents policyholders on roof claims across Dallas, Dallas County, and surrounding North Texas communities. We can inspect the roof, review the policy and endorsements, gather weather data, prepare a detailed estimate, and present the claim to the insurer. We do not perform repairs on claims we adjust, and we cannot guarantee an outcome. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Steps to Take After Roof Damage</h2>
<ol>
<li>Photograph the roof (from the ground if it is unsafe), ceilings, attic, and any debris.</li>
<li>Arrange temporary protection such as tarping, and keep receipts.</li>
<li>Report the claim promptly and write down the claim number and adjuster contact.</li>
<li>Request a copy of the insurer&rsquo;s estimate and inspection photos.</li>
<li>Avoid signing contracts or assignment documents you do not fully understand.</li>
</ol>
<p>For legal questions about a dispute, deadlines, or a lawsuit, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
